At this call site, `task` is of type `TaskModel`, and `getReferenceTaskName()` simply returns the backing field `referenceTaskName` without any null checks or transformations:

```java
public String getReferenceTaskName() {
    return referenceTaskName;
}
```

Across the provided code, there are many reads of `getReferenceTaskName()` but no global invariant that guarantees it is always non-null:

- `WorkflowModel.getTaskByRefName(String refName)` explicitly checks:
  ```java
  if (task.getReferenceTaskName() == null) {
      throw new RuntimeException("Task ... does not have reference name specified.");
  }
  ```
  This guard exists precisely because `getReferenceTaskName()` can be null on some `TaskModel` instances.

- `WorkflowExecutor.skipTaskFromWorkflow` creates a new `TaskModel` and calls:
  ```java
  taskToBeSkipped.setReferenceTaskName(taskReferenceName);
